1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
One sky. The ancient Chinese saw — five
sacred beasts. One is missing.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:08,000
China’s Four Symbols: one sacred beast
for each direction.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:11,000
But the centre is empty.

4
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:16,000
The Da Dai Liji: five kinds of animals,
each with a chief.

5
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Feathered: phoenix. Furred: qilin.
Shelled: sacred tortoise. Scaled:
jiaolong dragon.

6
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:26,500
The fifth: no feathers, no scales. Its
chief: the sage — a human.

7
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:33,500
Dragon, bird, tortoise match. No tiger —
the qilin instead. And one extra: a
human.

8
00:00:33,500 --> 00:00:40,500
The Book of Rites: the centre’s emperor
is the Yellow Emperor; its creatures,
the naked ones.

9
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:45,000
So which stars are the centre’s? Our
club lists three candidates.

10
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:50,500
One: Xuanyuan. The clue: it was the
Yellow Emperor’s name.

11
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:55,500
A winding chain around Leo: exactly 17
stars. Count them.

12
00:00:55,500 --> 00:01:02,500
Two: Vega and Altair — a rare sky story
about people. With Deneb: the Summer
Triangle.

13
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:08,500
Three: Hushi, the Bow. Five chariot
flags: four animals, one human-made bow.

14
00:01:08,500 --> 00:01:15,500
The Shiji: four stars called the Bow,
“facing the Wolf”. It aims at Sirius.

15
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:23,500
Three candidates, three question marks.
No surviving text says which asterism is
the centre’s.

16
00:01:23,500 --> 00:01:26,500
Orion: three stars the whole world
knows.

17
00:01:26,500 --> 00:01:29,500
Follow for a new sky every week.

